1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,350
Imagine you and I are both looking for an
apartment.

2
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,410
Suppose the app changes one apartment on
your list.

3
00:00:04,410 --> 00:00:06,270
Could that cost me the apartment I would
otherwise get?

4
00:00:06,270 --> 00:00:08,748
To see how that can happen, start with two
people and two seats.

5
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:12,850
Let's start with two applicants and two
school seats.

6
00:00:12,850 --> 00:00:15,790
A prefers school x to school y. B accepts
only x.

7
00:00:15,790 --> 00:00:17,427
And school x gives A priority over B.

8
00:00:17,607 --> 00:00:20,782
At first, A sees y and B sees x. Both get
a seat.

9
00:00:20,962 --> 00:00:24,517
Now change only A's recommendation. Show A
school x instead.

10
00:00:24,697 --> 00:00:28,817
Both applicants still see just one option.
But now they compete for the same seat.

11
00:00:28,817 --> 00:00:31,282
A gets x, B gets nothing, and y sits
empty.

12
00:00:31,462 --> 00:00:34,062
From A's perspective, the recommendation
got better.

13
00:00:34,062 --> 00:00:36,395
B's shortlist did not change at all, yet B
lost a place.

14
00:00:36,575 --> 00:00:39,355
Imagine evaluating that change with two
separate surveys.

15
00:00:39,355 --> 00:00:41,775
A could report that the app finally showed
the school they wanted.

16
00:00:41,775 --> 00:00:44,055
B could report that the screen looked
exactly the same.

17
00:00:44,055 --> 00:00:45,100
Both accounts would be accurate.

18
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:48,050
You would still have missed the empty seat
and the person who used to have one.

19
00:00:48,230 --> 00:00:50,350
That's the interaction we want to
understand.

20
00:00:50,350 --> 00:00:52,898
We would miss it if we evaluated each
person's recommendations separately.

21
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:57,190
First, a recommender chooses a short list
of feasible options.

22
00:00:57,190 --> 00:00:59,060
Then an assignment rule decides who gets
which place.

23
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:02,720
We keep track of three outcomes. Placement
means receiving any place.

24
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:05,232
Coverage means that a particular reference
choice appears on the shortlist.

25
00:01:05,412 --> 00:01:07,152
And an exit means someone has a place
under

26
00:01:07,152 --> 00:01:09,070
the comparison policy but loses it under
the new one.

27
00:01:09,250 --> 00:01:12,790
A coverage hit is easy to score from a
person's list once the reference is known.

28
00:01:12,790 --> 00:01:15,390
A placement is different. You need all the
submitted lists,

29
00:01:15,390 --> 00:01:18,429
the capacity of each option, and the rule
that decides among competing applicants.

30
00:01:18,609 --> 00:01:20,369
That's why we rerun the allocation with
everyone

31
00:01:20,369 --> 00:01:21,943
in the market whenever we change a policy.

32
00:01:22,123 --> 00:01:24,723
For rentals, the reference is the
apartment assigned when every feasible

33
00:01:24,723 --> 00:01:28,048
option is available. For Chile, it's the
school recorded in later enrollment.

34
00:01:28,228 --> 00:01:30,428
We want to improve recommendations while
keeping every

35
00:01:30,428 --> 00:01:33,208
baseline recipient matched somewhere. They
can switch schools.

36
00:01:33,208 --> 00:01:35,168
That is a weaker promise than keeping the
same school,

37
00:01:35,168 --> 00:01:36,361
or getting a school they like more.

38
00:01:38,350 --> 00:01:39,830
In our New York rental simulation,

39
00:01:39,830 --> 00:01:42,390
take the renters with only one to five
feasible apartments.

40
00:01:42,390 --> 00:01:43,870
Our policies already show them every
option,

41
00:01:43,870 --> 00:01:45,495
so their personal shortlists cannot
change.

42
00:01:45,675 --> 00:01:48,564
With the one-option anchor, their match
rate is 13.1 percent.

43
00:01:48,744 --> 00:01:52,515
With balanced shortlists across the
market, it rises to 27.5 percent.

44
00:01:52,695 --> 00:01:55,915
That's more than double, with exactly the
same choices on their own lists.

45
00:01:55,915 --> 00:01:57,681
Only other renters' recommendations
changed.

46
00:01:57,861 --> 00:02:00,361
Think of someone who can afford only three
of the listings.

47
00:02:00,361 --> 00:02:02,421
They already see all three. A prediction
about which of those

48
00:02:02,421 --> 00:02:04,952
apartments they want cannot explain a
change in their match here.

49
00:02:05,132 --> 00:02:07,852
To find the explanation, we have to look
at who else is applying.

50
00:02:08,032 --> 00:02:11,252
We held the apartments, renters and
assignment rule fixed across both runs.

51
00:02:11,252 --> 00:02:13,712
That lets us see how much the other
renters' lists can matter.

52
00:02:13,712 --> 00:02:16,611
The next comparison asks whether the same
thing can happen in school admissions.

53
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:21,570
For rentals, we start with the apartment
lists that GPT suggested.

54
00:02:21,570 --> 00:02:24,250
The listings and household profiles come
from market and survey data,

55
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:26,644
while preferences, landlord priorities and
assignments are modeled.

56
00:02:26,824 --> 00:02:29,424
We reconstruct 1,280 markets.

57
00:02:29,424 --> 00:02:32,021
In each one, the two policies face the
same renters and apartments.

58
00:02:32,201 --> 00:02:35,161
We also pair the random draws across
policies. Otherwise,

59
00:02:35,161 --> 00:02:37,305
a lucky set of preferences could look like
an algorithmic improvement.

60
00:02:37,485 --> 00:02:40,425
For the follow-up, we resample twenty seed
blocks ten thousand times,

61
00:02:40,425 --> 00:02:42,505
keeping all policies together within each
draw.

62
00:02:42,505 --> 00:02:44,561
Renters sharing a market are not
independent observations.

63
00:02:44,741 --> 00:02:46,561
We analyzed these three periods before,

64
00:02:46,561 --> 00:02:48,241
and some of the same apartments appear
again.

65
00:02:48,241 --> 00:02:50,388
The finding tells us how different
policies behave on those markets.

66
00:02:50,568 --> 00:02:53,508
There is also a supply limit. Of the
markets originally planned,

67
00:02:53,508 --> 00:02:56,348
320 could not be built under the Staten
Island supply requirements.

68
00:02:56,348 --> 00:02:57,642
We kept those failures in the accounting.

69
00:02:57,822 --> 00:03:00,062
The enriched sample asks what a
recommender can do when

70
00:03:00,062 --> 00:03:02,222
there are options to allocate. A household
with no feasible

71
00:03:02,222 --> 00:03:04,304
apartment cannot gain access through a
better ranking alone.

72
00:03:04,484 --> 00:03:07,884
Chile gives us families' submitted
rankings and actual admissions rules.

73
00:03:07,884 --> 00:03:09,604
Our reconstruction matches the released
course or no-offer

74
00:03:09,604 --> 00:03:11,346
outcomes for about two million
applicant-years.

75
00:03:11,526 --> 00:03:14,246
In a separate 2021 entry-grade replay,

76
00:03:14,246 --> 00:03:18,557
237 of 309 net lost placements fall among
pupils whose own shortlists stay fixed.

77
00:03:18,737 --> 00:03:21,977
That is a counterfactual comparison, not a
school-advice trial.

78
00:03:21,977 --> 00:03:23,893
The later cohorts we'll discuss are
counterfactuals too.

79
00:03:24,073 --> 00:03:26,193
Both settings let us ask how other
people's recommendations

80
00:03:26,193 --> 00:03:28,510
change your access, using different kinds
of evidence.

81
00:03:30,350 --> 00:03:33,410
The first rule coordinates one option. We
call it the anchor.

82
00:03:33,410 --> 00:03:35,650
Find a maximum matching across all
feasible pairs.

83
00:03:35,650 --> 00:03:38,694
When several maximum matchings exist,
prefer pairs already on the shortlists.

84
00:03:38,874 --> 00:03:41,274
Then put each assigned option onto that
person's list,

85
00:03:41,274 --> 00:03:42,495
replacing at most one option.

86
00:03:42,675 --> 00:03:44,915
The second rule coordinates the whole
shortlist.

87
00:03:44,915 --> 00:03:46,695
Count how often each listing is
recommended,

88
00:03:46,695 --> 00:03:48,495
and minimize the sum of those counts
squared.

89
00:03:48,495 --> 00:03:50,806
Concentrating recommendations on a few
listings becomes expensive.

90
00:03:50,986 --> 00:03:54,526
Among equally balanced solutions, favor
the saved GPT ranks.

91
00:03:54,526 --> 00:03:56,200
We solve this using standard min-cost
flow.

92
00:03:56,380 --> 00:03:59,280
There is a simple marginal cost behind
that optimization.

93
00:03:59,280 --> 00:04:01,620
The first recommendation to a listing
costs one unit.

94
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:03,260
The next costs three, then five.

95
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:05,055
Those are the differences between
successive squares.

96
00:04:05,235 --> 00:04:08,075
If two feasible apartments are equally
useful for our tie breaker,

97
00:04:08,075 --> 00:04:10,515
the algorithm has a reason to recommend
the less crowded one.

98
00:04:10,515 --> 00:04:11,735
Nobody has reserved it.

99
00:04:11,735 --> 00:04:14,091
Applicants still compete according to
preferences and priorities.

100
00:04:14,271 --> 00:04:16,511
Neither rule sees the realized preference
shocks,

101
00:04:16,511 --> 00:04:18,251
landlord rankings or reference
assignments.

102
00:04:18,251 --> 00:04:20,092
They use feasibility and saved
recommendations.

103
00:04:20,272 --> 00:04:22,432
There are two different matchings in this
experiment.

104
00:04:22,432 --> 00:04:24,692
The anchor matching tells the platform
which suggestions to add.

105
00:04:24,692 --> 00:04:25,872
It is a planning calculation.

106
00:04:25,872 --> 00:04:28,347
Then we run applicant-proposing deferred
acceptance on the shortlists.

107
00:04:28,527 --> 00:04:31,027
The person a planner earmarked for an
apartment can still lose it in

108
00:04:31,027 --> 00:04:34,017
the assignment. That gap is exactly where
a retention question arises.

109
00:04:34,197 --> 00:04:36,397
Both keep each person's list length fixed.

110
00:04:36,397 --> 00:04:39,697
But balancing can replace several options,
while the anchor replaces at most one.

111
00:04:39,697 --> 00:04:41,894
Neither rule explicitly protects existing
recipients.

112
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:45,930
Before adding coordination, look at GPT
against random shortlists

113
00:04:45,930 --> 00:04:48,510
of the same length. Blue shows the
coverage difference.

114
00:04:48,510 --> 00:04:51,322
Orange shows the placement difference.
Zero would mean no change from random.

115
00:04:51,502 --> 00:04:54,842
Across the three enriched calendar
windows, GPT gains about eight percentage

116
00:04:54,842 --> 00:04:57,462
points of coverage and loses about two to
four points of placement.

117
00:04:57,462 --> 00:05:00,287
The directions repeat, and the original
market-bootstrap intervals exclude zero.

118
00:05:00,467 --> 00:05:02,787
Those original error bars resample
markets.

119
00:05:02,787 --> 00:05:05,387
Our follow-up uses paired seed-block
intervals, including a simultaneous

120
00:05:05,387 --> 00:05:07,929
ninety-five percent band across four
comparisons and two outcomes.

121
00:05:08,109 --> 00:05:11,549
We use the largest deviation across those
eight estimates to set a common margin.

122
00:05:11,549 --> 00:05:13,109
This addresses the extra chances to find a
favorable

123
00:05:13,109 --> 00:05:15,027
result when looking at several comparisons
together.

124
00:05:15,207 --> 00:05:18,107
So the lists are better at including the
full-list reference assignment.

125
00:05:18,107 --> 00:05:19,467
But that doesn't translate into more
people getting

126
00:05:19,467 --> 00:05:20,808
apartments when the lists are used
together.

127
00:05:20,988 --> 00:05:23,228
These enriched markets deliberately
include affordable listings

128
00:05:23,228 --> 00:05:26,248
and renters with feasible options. We also
report the ordinary

129
00:05:26,248 --> 00:05:28,499
sampling design and the uncertainty
details in the appendix.

130
00:05:30,350 --> 00:05:32,530
Now put the four policies on the same
graph.

131
00:05:32,530 --> 00:05:34,470
Moving right means more reference
coverage.

132
00:05:34,470 --> 00:05:35,821
Moving up means more people placed.

133
00:05:36,001 --> 00:05:39,221
Starting from random, GPT moves right but
slightly down.

134
00:05:39,221 --> 00:05:41,703
Adding the anchor moves GPT upward, with a
small coverage change.

135
00:05:41,883 --> 00:05:43,763
Balancing moves much farther up.

136
00:05:43,763 --> 00:05:46,183
Compared with the anchor, it gains another
12.6 percentage points

137
00:05:46,183 --> 00:05:48,297
of placement and loses about three points
of coverage in this panel.

138
00:05:48,477 --> 00:05:51,457
But compare balancing with random, and
both outcomes improve.

139
00:05:51,457 --> 00:05:54,057
So this isn't an unavoidable conflict
between coverage and placement.

140
00:05:54,057 --> 00:05:55,575
It depends on which policies we compare.

141
00:05:55,755 --> 00:05:57,275
A stronger control is random
recommendations

142
00:05:57,275 --> 00:05:58,427
with the same anchoring rule.

143
00:05:58,607 --> 00:06:01,867
In this panel, anchored GPT places only
seven more renter appearances,

144
00:06:01,867 --> 00:06:03,247
out of ten thousand two hundred forty,

145
00:06:03,247 --> 00:06:05,206
and the paired interval for the placement
difference crosses zero.

146
00:06:05,386 --> 00:06:08,306
GPT improves reference coverage against
that control;

147
00:06:08,306 --> 00:06:10,258
we cannot attribute a clear placement gain
to GPT.

148
00:06:10,438 --> 00:06:13,398
The comparison between balancing and
anchoring was more consistent.

149
00:06:13,398 --> 00:06:16,218
Across all six combinations of calendar
window and sampling design,

150
00:06:16,218 --> 00:06:17,967
balancing raised placement and lowered
reference coverage.

151
00:06:18,147 --> 00:06:21,067
The simultaneous intervals for those two
differences excluded zero in

152
00:06:21,067 --> 00:06:24,507
every combination. That is a pattern
across these simulations. It is still

153
00:06:24,507 --> 00:06:26,932
possible to improve both outcomes when the
starting rule is different.

154
00:06:27,112 --> 00:06:30,152
These are means for the September and
October enriched panel.

155
00:06:30,152 --> 00:06:32,532
A score-based control, with extra
information from the simulation,

156
00:06:32,532 --> 00:06:35,951
improves both measures further. We haven't
established GPT as the best recommender.

157
00:06:36,131 --> 00:06:38,080
Now, who gains or loses a match?

158
00:06:39,350 --> 00:06:42,732
Compare the anchor with the original GPT
lists, on the same rental markets.

159
00:06:42,912 --> 00:06:45,477
First, 449 renter appearances gain a
place.

160
00:06:45,657 --> 00:06:47,337
Then there are 37 exits:

161
00:06:47,337 --> 00:06:49,823
appearances that had a place under GPT and
lose it under the anchor.

162
00:06:50,003 --> 00:06:52,835
Subtract those losses and the net gain is
412 placements.

163
00:06:53,015 --> 00:06:54,795
The denominator is ten thousand two
hundred

164
00:06:54,795 --> 00:06:56,715
forty simulated renter appearances.

165
00:06:56,715 --> 00:06:59,355
We also check what happens beyond matched
versus unmatched: two hundred

166
00:06:59,355 --> 00:07:01,915
seventy-five appearances receive a worse
modeled rank under the anchor.

167
00:07:02,095 --> 00:07:04,315
These measures can overlap. Preserving a
place and preserving

168
00:07:04,315 --> 00:07:06,089
the quality of that place are different
promises.

169
00:07:06,269 --> 00:07:09,469
Reporting just 412 would leave out the
people who lost access.

170
00:07:09,469 --> 00:07:11,909
Balancing has the same issue: it places
many more people,

171
00:07:11,909 --> 00:07:14,140
but causes 171 exits relative to the
anchor.

172
00:07:14,320 --> 00:07:17,780
Someone who appears in two simulated
markets contributes two records.

173
00:07:17,780 --> 00:07:20,410
An exit here means a change between
assignments on the same simulated market.

174
00:07:22,350 --> 00:07:24,390
Now return to the group in our opening
result:

175
00:07:24,390 --> 00:07:27,490
renters with one to five feasible
apartments. They already see everything.

176
00:07:27,490 --> 00:07:29,321
Neither coordination rule can change their
own shortlist.

177
00:07:29,501 --> 00:07:32,501
Their match rate rises from 13.1 to 27.5
percent when

178
00:07:32,501 --> 00:07:34,461
the rest of the market moves from
anchoring to balancing.

179
00:07:34,461 --> 00:07:37,753
Across this subgroup, 349 appearances gain
a match and 15 lose one.

180
00:07:37,933 --> 00:07:41,193
This subgroup contains two thousand three
hundred twenty-eight renter appearances.

181
00:07:41,193 --> 00:07:42,853
In raw counts, the number matched goes
from

182
00:07:42,853 --> 00:07:44,242
three hundred four to six hundred
thirty-eight.

183
00:07:44,422 --> 00:07:46,642
The displayed percentages average
seed-level rates,

184
00:07:46,642 --> 00:07:48,748
so those averages and the pooled counts
use different weighting.

185
00:07:48,928 --> 00:07:51,388
The middle window's pointwise interval for
the gain runs from about

186
00:07:51,388 --> 00:07:55,683
13.3 to 15.5 percentage points. Across
windows, some properties recur.

187
00:07:55,863 --> 00:07:58,623
Repeating a result with those listings is
informative about the mechanism,

188
00:07:58,623 --> 00:07:59,803
but it would be a mistake to read the
three

189
00:07:59,803 --> 00:08:01,773
estimates as three independent housing
interventions.

190
00:08:01,953 --> 00:08:03,553
In the three enriched rental windows,

191
00:08:03,553 --> 00:08:06,334
the subgroup gains between 14.4 and 14.9
percentage points.

192
00:08:06,514 --> 00:08:08,334
Because their own choices stay fixed,

193
00:08:08,334 --> 00:08:10,634
those changes must come through
competition with other renters.

194
00:08:10,634 --> 00:08:12,499
We can look for the same issue when people
compete for jobs.

195
00:08:14,350 --> 00:08:15,990
Consider the jobs replication.

196
00:08:15,990 --> 00:08:18,910
We use New York metro postings and
survey-based seeker profiles.

197
00:08:18,910 --> 00:08:21,270
In each simulated market, people see up to
five feasible jobs.

198
00:08:21,450 --> 00:08:24,850
The experiment pairs the same seekers and
postings across policies.

199
00:08:24,850 --> 00:08:26,714
The outcome is the assignment in that
simulation.

200
00:08:26,894 --> 00:08:30,414
With GPT shortlists, 63.5 percent get a
placement.

201
00:08:30,414 --> 00:08:34,054
Coordinate just one option on each list,
and that rises to 79.4 percent.

202
00:08:34,054 --> 00:08:37,784
That is 612 additional placements on net
across 3,840 seeker appearances.

203
00:08:37,963 --> 00:08:40,684
Then apply the same anchoring rule to
random shortlists.

204
00:08:40,684 --> 00:08:42,983
That simple control reaches 81.4 percent.

205
00:08:42,983 --> 00:08:44,551
Coordination explains a great deal of the
recovery.

206
00:08:44,731 --> 00:08:47,510
This comparison gives us no placement
advantage to claim for the language model.

207
00:08:47,690 --> 00:08:50,230
Here is what we can learn even with only
the published totals.

208
00:08:50,230 --> 00:08:52,350
The anchor changed 920 personal lists.

209
00:08:52,350 --> 00:08:54,070
Under the fixed priorities in this jobs
model,

210
00:08:54,070 --> 00:08:56,200
changing one list can create at most one
new recipient.

211
00:08:56,380 --> 00:08:59,240
To see why, remove that seeker, then add
them back.

212
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:02,160
Rejections travel down a single chain.
Several people can switch jobs,

213
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:04,237
but only one person at a time is looking
for a vacancy.

214
00:09:04,417 --> 00:09:07,297
So there can be at most 920 entrants
across these edits.

215
00:09:07,297 --> 00:09:10,037
Entrants minus exits equals the net gain
of 612.

216
00:09:10,037 --> 00:09:12,137
Subtraction gives an upper bound of 308
exits,

217
00:09:12,137 --> 00:09:13,876
and zero is also consistent with the
bounds.

218
00:09:14,056 --> 00:09:15,936
We cannot tell which individuals lost a
match

219
00:09:15,936 --> 00:09:18,136
or measure coverage after the anchor from
these totals.

220
00:09:18,136 --> 00:09:20,527
Chile's individual application records let
us follow that question further.

221
00:09:22,350 --> 00:09:25,190
In Chile, families tell us which schools
they want and in what order. We can

222
00:09:25,190 --> 00:09:27,607
hold those rankings fixed as we change the
shortlists and run admissions again.

223
00:09:27,787 --> 00:09:31,427
We compare two classifiers trained on an
earlier cohort to predict initial offers.

224
00:09:31,427 --> 00:09:33,227
One uses submitted rank; the other
doesn't.

225
00:09:33,227 --> 00:09:34,766
They choose shortlists for later cohorts.

226
00:09:34,946 --> 00:09:37,992
We then check coverage of later enrollment
and rerun admissions on those shortlists.

227
00:09:38,172 --> 00:09:41,772
The rank-informed policy gains 4,638
enrollment hits overall,

228
00:09:41,772 --> 00:09:44,652
but loses 1,670 placements compared with
the rank-blind policy.

229
00:09:44,832 --> 00:09:48,172
The graph separates pupils by whether they
historically received an initial offer.

230
00:09:48,172 --> 00:09:49,756
Each bar has its own group's denominator.

231
00:09:49,936 --> 00:09:52,656
Pupils with no initial offer are 7.4
percent of the linked sample,

232
00:09:52,656 --> 00:09:54,581
but account for 76 percent of the net
placement loss.

233
00:09:54,761 --> 00:09:57,701
That is 1,270 net lost placements in the
smaller group,

234
00:09:57,701 --> 00:09:59,746
a decline of 0.873 percentage points.

235
00:09:59,926 --> 00:10:03,266
Those losses come from comparing two
simulated assignments for the same pupils.

236
00:10:03,266 --> 00:10:04,586
They fall disproportionately on families
who went

237
00:10:04,586 --> 00:10:06,183
through the original admissions round
without an offer.

238
00:10:06,363 --> 00:10:09,463
The four cohorts contribute just under two
million linked applicant-years.

239
00:10:09,463 --> 00:10:12,910
The net loss combines 2,397 exits and 727
entries.

240
00:10:13,090 --> 00:10:14,850
A study observing only how many children
got seats

241
00:10:14,850 --> 00:10:16,529
would miss those movements in opposite
directions.

242
00:10:18,350 --> 00:10:19,590
Keep the rank-informed policy,

243
00:10:19,590 --> 00:10:21,786
but compare it with each family's first
five discretionary courses.

244
00:10:21,966 --> 00:10:27,006
Now both totals improve: 9,800 more
enrollment hits and 8,143 more placements.

245
00:10:27,006 --> 00:10:29,336
Once again, the prediction–placement
conflict depends on the comparison.

246
00:10:29,516 --> 00:10:31,496
Yet pupils without a historical initial
offer still

247
00:10:31,496 --> 00:10:33,907
lose 2,860 placements against this
alternative.

248
00:10:34,087 --> 00:10:35,887
A policy can look better in aggregate and
still make

249
00:10:35,887 --> 00:10:37,525
access worse for people we especially
hoped to help.

250
00:10:37,705 --> 00:10:39,305
There is a timing detail here.

251
00:10:39,305 --> 00:10:41,625
We identify the no-offer group using what
happened historically,

252
00:10:41,625 --> 00:10:44,064
after fixing the policies. It helps us
audit who bore a loss.

253
00:10:44,244 --> 00:10:46,164
It would give a new policy information
from the future if we

254
00:10:46,164 --> 00:10:48,284
used that outcome to choose whom to
protect before assignment.

255
00:10:48,464 --> 00:10:50,504
It also reminds us that matching
historical enrollment isn't

256
00:10:50,504 --> 00:10:52,904
the same as improving welfare. To move
beyond those averages,

257
00:10:52,904 --> 00:10:54,760
we need a precise requirement for who
should remain protected.

258
00:10:56,350 --> 00:10:58,520
We can write that requirement as a design
problem.

259
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:01,640
Fix the baseline shortlists and a supplied
set of priority draws.

260
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:05,000
Choose one new policy that works across
every draw. We want more reference hits,

261
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,372
with every baseline recipient still
getting a seat on each draw.

262
00:11:07,552 --> 00:11:09,830
They may switch schools, but they cannot
become unplaced.

263
00:11:10,010 --> 00:11:11,190
For the Chile application,

264
00:11:11,190 --> 00:11:14,010
we keep options shared by the rank-blind
and rank-informed policies,

265
00:11:14,010 --> 00:11:17,087
choose the remaining options from their
union, and preserve each list's length.

266
00:11:17,267 --> 00:11:20,087
Deciding whether a target gain is
achievable is NP-complete,

267
00:11:20,087 --> 00:11:22,727
even with unit seats and lists of at most
three options.

268
00:11:22,727 --> 00:11:25,228
Checking a proposed policy with deferred
acceptance is still polynomial.

269
00:11:25,408 --> 00:11:28,088
The certificate is simply the proposed
shortlists.

270
00:11:28,088 --> 00:11:30,548
We run deferred acceptance on every
supplied draw, compare each

271
00:11:30,548 --> 00:11:32,655
recipient with the corresponding baseline,
and count the hits.

272
00:11:32,835 --> 00:11:36,355
The shortlists stay fixed across draws;
the resulting assignments can change.

273
00:11:36,355 --> 00:11:37,635
That is why verification is efficient,

274
00:11:37,635 --> 00:11:39,267
even though finding the best policy is
hard.

275
00:11:39,447 --> 00:11:42,907
This theorem uses fixed, strict priorities
on an explicit panel.

276
00:11:42,907 --> 00:11:45,067
We test Chile's family-linked rules
separately.

277
00:11:45,067 --> 00:11:47,581
The next picture explains where the
computational difficulty comes from.

278
00:11:49,350 --> 00:11:50,550
Start with a graph.

279
00:11:50,550 --> 00:11:53,710
Each vertex represents an edit that adds
one reference hit. For each edge,

280
00:11:53,710 --> 00:11:56,333
we construct a priority draw where its two
endpoint edits can interfere.

281
00:11:56,513 --> 00:11:59,093
On this draw, choosing edit u displaces
one already-matched

282
00:11:59,093 --> 00:12:01,775
pupil toward the fallback seat. That edit
alone is safe.

283
00:12:01,955 --> 00:12:04,155
Now choose edit v as well.

284
00:12:04,155 --> 00:12:07,151
A second pupil needs the same fallback
seat, and one of them must lose a place.

285
00:12:07,331 --> 00:12:09,719
Other vertex edits are harmless on this
particular draw.

286
00:12:09,899 --> 00:12:12,079
We repeat this construction for every
edge.

287
00:12:12,079 --> 00:12:14,079
One fixed set of edits must work on every
draw,

288
00:12:14,079 --> 00:12:16,479
so it cannot contain both endpoints of any
edge.

289
00:12:16,479 --> 00:12:18,188
The safe choices form an independent set.

290
00:12:18,368 --> 00:12:20,228
Each chosen edit adds one hit.

291
00:12:20,228 --> 00:12:22,365
Maximizing safe gains would therefore
solve Independent Set.

292
00:12:22,545 --> 00:12:24,965
The construction also includes a reference
draw.

293
00:12:24,965 --> 00:12:27,405
Running full-list deferred acceptance
there generates the historical targets

294
00:12:27,405 --> 00:12:30,344
themselves, so the reduction doesn't rely
on arbitrary target labels.

295
00:12:30,524 --> 00:12:33,584
There is one conflict draw per graph edge,
plus that one reference draw,

296
00:12:33,584 --> 00:12:35,904
so the instance grows polynomially. A safe
gain of at least

297
00:12:35,904 --> 00:12:38,981
K exists exactly when K mutually
nonadjacent vertices exist.

298
00:12:39,161 --> 00:12:41,401
The same construction tells us something
else.

299
00:12:41,401 --> 00:12:44,081
Imagine a graph made of disjoint edges.
Keeping everyone's exact

300
00:12:44,081 --> 00:12:46,682
original assignment allows zero selected
edits on the reference draw.

301
00:12:46,862 --> 00:12:50,082
Keeping everyone seated lets us choose one
endpoint from each edge.

302
00:12:50,082 --> 00:12:53,222
With more edges, the gap can grow as large
as we like. So an exact-assignment

303
00:12:53,222 --> 00:12:56,039
ceiling cannot automatically be used as a
ceiling for seat retention.

304
00:12:57,350 --> 00:13:00,290
Back in Chile, we compare two promises on
the same design panels:

305
00:13:00,290 --> 00:13:03,826
1,024 lotteries per cohort, for 2022
through 2024.

306
00:13:04,006 --> 00:13:06,286
The stricter promise keeps every baseline
recipient in the same

307
00:13:06,286 --> 00:13:08,486
course and seat type. Within our allowed
shortlist family,

308
00:13:08,486 --> 00:13:11,027
the maximum additional enrollment coverage
is exactly 314 hits.

309
00:13:11,207 --> 00:13:14,207
The looser promise lets people switch
schools, as long as everyone keeps a seat.

310
00:13:14,207 --> 00:13:18,619
We construct shortlists that gain 642
hits. The certified upper bound is 4,620.

311
00:13:18,799 --> 00:13:21,219
Allowing switches gives us more than twice
the demonstrated gain.

312
00:13:21,219 --> 00:13:22,659
But there's a wide gap to the upper bound.

313
00:13:22,659 --> 00:13:24,060
We haven't solved this problem to
optimality.

314
00:13:24,240 --> 00:13:26,880
The new shortlist must keep courses shared
by the rank-blind and rank-informed

315
00:13:26,880 --> 00:13:30,160
policies. Its remaining slots come from
the union of those two original lists.

316
00:13:30,160 --> 00:13:31,162
Its length stays fixed.

317
00:13:31,342 --> 00:13:33,562
That constraint makes the comparison
interpretable.

318
00:13:33,562 --> 00:13:36,442
It also sets the scope of the number 314.
A platform allowed to add

319
00:13:36,442 --> 00:13:38,647
arbitrary new courses would face a
different optimization problem.

320
00:13:38,827 --> 00:13:41,247
The gap measures what the optimization has
left unresolved.

321
00:13:41,247 --> 00:13:42,647
We have a concrete policy for the lower
endpoint

322
00:13:42,647 --> 00:13:44,188
and a mathematical bound for the upper
endpoint.

323
00:13:44,368 --> 00:13:46,248
Protection covers every baseline
recipient,

324
00:13:46,248 --> 00:13:48,048
including those without an enrollment
link.

325
00:13:48,048 --> 00:13:49,517
Only the coverage objective needs that
link.

326
00:13:49,697 --> 00:13:51,237
These are hindsight benchmarks:

327
00:13:51,237 --> 00:13:54,097
they use the evaluation cohorts' later
enrollment labels to choose edits.

328
00:13:54,097 --> 00:13:55,893
A real policy wouldn't have those labels
before assignment.

329
00:13:56,073 --> 00:13:58,731
So we also test rules using only
information available at that point.

330
00:14:00,350 --> 00:14:01,790
Here are two such rules.

331
00:14:01,790 --> 00:14:04,490
We freeze them after applications arrive
and before the ordinary assignment.

332
00:14:04,490 --> 00:14:07,425
Neither uses that cohort's realized offers
or later enrollment to select shortlists.

333
00:14:07,605 --> 00:14:09,905
The structural rule restricts interactions
around applicants without

334
00:14:09,905 --> 00:14:12,985
a guaranteed fallback. Its proof covers
all permitted ordinary lottery orders,

335
00:14:12,985 --> 00:14:14,576
for the fixed inputs and reconstructed
mechanism.

336
00:14:14,756 --> 00:14:17,836
The proof starts with common options that
already guarantee a pupil a seat.

337
00:14:17,836 --> 00:14:19,336
Around pupils lacking that guarantee,

338
00:14:19,336 --> 00:14:21,596
it freezes the relevant competitors and
family interactions.

339
00:14:21,596 --> 00:14:22,767
This limits where edits can propagate.

340
00:14:22,947 --> 00:14:25,727
We keep the same three cohorts and draw
fresh priorities.

341
00:14:25,727 --> 00:14:28,907
There are 384 course-level cohort draws
and 96 cohort draws using school

342
00:14:28,907 --> 00:14:31,971
and family tickets. This tests new lottery
orders for the same applicants.

343
00:14:32,151 --> 00:14:35,212
It gains 28 enrollment hits and has zero
exits in the test panels.

344
00:14:35,392 --> 00:14:38,252
The learned rule passes its design-panel
checks and gains 50 hits.

345
00:14:38,252 --> 00:14:40,277
But it has one exit in each of two fresh
test panels.

346
00:14:40,457 --> 00:14:42,057
Twenty-eight is a small gain.

347
00:14:42,057 --> 00:14:43,897
What matters here is the difference
between a proof covering the allowed

348
00:14:43,897 --> 00:14:46,397
orders and a rule that happened to pass
many simulated draws.

349
00:14:46,397 --> 00:14:48,254
More testing alone doesn't turn the second
into the first.

350
00:14:50,350 --> 00:14:53,070
The retention guarantee still leaves a
social-impact question.

351
00:14:53,070 --> 00:14:55,178
Protecting people who already have a place
can leave everyone else out.

352
00:14:55,358 --> 00:14:57,458
Our structural Chile rule has no new
entrants on the reported

353
00:14:57,458 --> 00:14:59,938
paired test lotteries. It keeps baseline
recipients placed,

354
00:14:59,938 --> 00:15:02,290
but doesn't expand access for those who
were unplaced under the baseline.

355
00:15:02,470 --> 00:15:04,570
So the next objective must count both
groups:

356
00:15:04,570 --> 00:15:06,789
the people we protect and the people still
waiting for an opportunity.

357
00:15:06,969 --> 00:15:09,769
For a school district, that would mean
reporting two columns at minimum.

358
00:15:09,769 --> 00:15:11,589
Of those who would have received a seat
under the old policy,

359
00:15:11,589 --> 00:15:12,569
how many still get one?

360
00:15:12,569 --> 00:15:14,428
Of those who would have gone without, how
many now have a seat?

361
00:15:14,608 --> 00:15:17,384
A rule can score perfectly in the first
column while the second stays at zero.

362
00:15:17,564 --> 00:15:20,224
How people would act on our
recommendations is an open question.

363
00:15:20,224 --> 00:15:22,524
They may ignore advice or add choices.
Before deployment,

364
00:15:22,524 --> 00:15:24,865
they should be able to correct mistakes
and seek human review or appeal.

365
00:15:25,045 --> 00:15:27,437
Those are proposed safeguards, not
interventions we've tested.

366
00:15:27,617 --> 00:15:29,477
And keeping a seat may mean a worse
school.

367
00:15:29,477 --> 00:15:31,665
Historical enrollment and modeled ranks
aren't complete welfare measures.

368
00:15:31,845 --> 00:15:33,845
Recommendations can redistribute
opportunities,

369
00:15:33,845 --> 00:15:35,892
but they cannot create affordable
apartments or school capacity.

370
00:15:36,072 --> 00:15:38,312
Even the structural rule's coverage gain
is a net figure.

371
00:15:38,312 --> 00:15:41,352
In 115 applicant records the historical
school appears for the first time.

372
00:15:41,352 --> 00:15:44,372
In 87 it disappears. None of those losses
violates seat retention.

373
00:15:44,552 --> 00:15:46,612
The mathematical promise protects
placement status;

374
00:15:46,612 --> 00:15:49,320
it does not protect every useful option or
every pupil's preference satisfaction.

375
00:15:51,350 --> 00:15:54,230
In a 2021 Chilean field experiment by Agte
and colleagues,

376
00:15:54,230 --> 00:15:56,390
families were randomly assigned different
versions of a school map.

377
00:15:56,390 --> 00:15:57,609
The basic version showed the schools.

378
00:15:57,789 --> 00:15:59,969
Another version gave counts by price and
quality and highlighted

379
00:15:59,969 --> 00:16:02,709
inexpensive schools with medium or high
official quality ratings.

380
00:16:02,709 --> 00:16:03,652
Families could apply anywhere.

381
00:16:03,832 --> 00:16:05,652
Compared with the basic map, the
highlighted version

382
00:16:05,652 --> 00:16:07,792
raised application submission by three
percentage points.

383
00:16:07,792 --> 00:16:10,522
It raised the share ranking a qualifying
school second by 5.4 points.

384
00:16:10,702 --> 00:16:13,562
But the estimate for receiving any
assignment was only 0.2 points,

385
00:16:13,562 --> 00:16:16,422
with an approximate interval from minus
two to plus 2.4 points.

386
00:16:16,422 --> 00:16:18,106
That leaves both gains and losses
plausible.

387
00:16:18,286 --> 00:16:21,846
The outcomes use different samples. A
qualifying second choice met the criteria.

388
00:16:21,846 --> 00:16:24,146
We don't know whether that particular
school appeared on that family's map.

389
00:16:24,146 --> 00:16:26,247
The trial establishes that families can
respond to information.

390
00:16:26,427 --> 00:16:28,888
It doesn't tell us what would happen if
everyone received our shortlists.

391
00:16:30,350 --> 00:16:32,970
Here is a reason to care about that last
distinction.

392
00:16:32,970 --> 00:16:35,030
Take a market with three pupils and three
seats.

393
00:16:35,030 --> 00:16:38,470
The old rule shows the first two pupils
school X, and the third pupil school Y.

394
00:16:38,470 --> 00:16:39,796
The new rule points everyone to Y.

395
00:16:39,976 --> 00:16:43,695
At Y, the second pupil has top priority,
followed by the first and then the third.

396
00:16:43,875 --> 00:16:45,895
Imagine that each pupil independently has

397
00:16:45,895 --> 00:16:47,975
a one-in-four chance of receiving the new
rule.

398
00:16:47,975 --> 00:16:50,834
For the first pupil, switching hurts only
when the second pupil also switches.

399
00:16:51,014 --> 00:16:53,494
For the second pupil, switching helps when
the first pupil stays

400
00:16:53,494 --> 00:16:56,734
with X and takes that seat. The third
pupil sees Y either way.

401
00:16:56,734 --> 00:16:59,914
That gives individual effects of minus one
quarter, plus three quarters, and zero.

402
00:17:00,094 --> 00:17:01,947
Their average is positive, one sixth.

403
00:17:02,127 --> 00:17:05,987
Now switch everyone. Under the old rule, X
and Y each filled a seat.

404
00:17:05,987 --> 00:17:08,976
Under the new rule, everyone competes at
Y. Only one seat fills.

405
00:17:09,156 --> 00:17:11,556
A positive direct effect in a small trial
coexists

406
00:17:11,556 --> 00:17:13,647
with a drop from two placements to one at
full rollout.

407
00:17:13,826 --> 00:17:16,426
A field test of the whole policy should
therefore track everyone sharing

408
00:17:16,426 --> 00:17:18,693
those seats, including families who never
changed their own list.

409
00:17:20,350 --> 00:17:22,630
A shortlist reaches beyond the person
receiving it.

410
00:17:22,630 --> 00:17:24,563
It changes who competes for the available
places.

411
00:17:24,743 --> 00:17:27,963
Our experiments make that interaction
visible. Our theory asks what it

412
00:17:27,963 --> 00:17:30,877
takes to improve recommendations while
protecting existing recipients.

413
00:17:31,057 --> 00:17:32,917
Coordination can improve the totals.

414
00:17:32,917 --> 00:17:35,277
The harder design question is how to open
opportunities for people without

415
00:17:35,277 --> 00:17:38,697
a place while keeping those who already
have one matched somewhere. Thank you.

